WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:06.100
January 2020, a pound of ground
coffee in America cost $4.17.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.880
Last month, that same pound cost $9.33.

3
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:11.730
Same cans, same beans.

4
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.270
Same sad little scoop.

5
00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.400
The price more than doubled and somehow
the coffee did not get twice as good.

6
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.550
Normally when something gets that
expensive, people just buy less of it.

7
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.860
That's kind of the whole point of prices.

8
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.750
Coffee is what economists
call an inelastic good,

9
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.300
which means when the price goes up,
people complain, they post about it,

10
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.650
and then they buy it anyway, because the
alternative is being awake without it.

11
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.350
And this isn't a vibe.

12
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.990
This is the government's own price data,
and it goes all the way back to 1980.

13
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.300
For 40 years, a pound of coffee
never cost more than about $6.

14
00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.090
Then around 2021, the line does this.

15
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.760
In April, it hit $9.72, the highest
price in the entire data set.

16
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:53.880
So no, your coffee didn't change.

17
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.730
Same cans, same beans.

18
00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.800
The scoop, however, has
gotten a lot sadder.
